         <div type="edition" xml:lang="tel-Latn">
            <p>
               <lb n="1"/><gap reason="undefined" unit="character" quantity="3" precision="low"/><unclear>pa</unclear>nasa
               <lb n="2"/>para<unclear>du</unclear>laku <unclear>dī</unclear> <lb n="3" break="no"/>ni payi Anni<lb n="4" break="no"/>ya<unclear>ṁ</unclear>bu voḍicina<lb n="5" break="no"/>vanṟu baranasi vē<lb n="6" break="no"/>vuru paṟa <unclear>ḻa</unclear>ci<lb n="7" break="no"/>na <unclear>ca</unclear>ṟukaṭu 
               <!-- The reading must be corrected. In the last line it should read °na-v(ā)nṟu Agu. ca and va are often similar ("short ca" and "long va"), ka and A can be quite similar; but there is a problem with ṭu and gu because the latter has the °u towards the right while the former usually has it to the left. If the ga was written with a curled left end - as it is often met with - and the right slope was missing or blurred it could look like an unusual ṭu with the ° to the right. -->
            </p>
